A maintainer on the project EffortlessMetrics/cargo-allow (Rust) filed this issue about hand-rolled graph machinery causing pain:

Issue #3902: program(modernization): turn Rust, dependency, CI, package, and API claims into checked evidence

Current second-pass basis: `main@1f099729cce336ec9804153b1eb44c582684f6d4`.

Existing authorities reused rather than duplicated:

- repository controls: #2283 / #2284 / #3747;
- package/product topology: #2604 / #2922 / #2923 / #3697;
- supply-chain umbrella: #1786;
- cargo-allow release/support: #2371 / #2478 / #2501;
- package-family candidates: #3386 / #3387 / #3388;
- issue-to-merge operating pack: #2175 / #2254;
- CI performance/routing: #3835 / #2569.

## Purpose

The repository already uses a modern Rust and package baseline. The remaining modernization gap is not another tool collection. It is the distance between what manifests, lockfiles, docs, workflows, scripts, public APIs, hosted environments, and issue packets appear to promise and what the repository proves continuously.

This program turns those claims into bounded evidence:

```text
declared Rust/dependency/API/package/workflow/control contract
+ exact source, toolchain, feature, target, environment, and product identity
→ deterministic proof or explicit unsupported/partial posture
→ small PR-scoped drift guards
```

The program keeps cargo-allow, cargo-intent, cargo-proof, and the shared packages independent. A cargo-allow core release is not made contingent on every experimental sibling lane unless the selected package topology says that exact compatibility surface is part of the candidate.

## Current observations

At the second-pass basis:

- the workspace uses resolver 3, Edition 2024, Rust 1.95, product-scoped versions, and tuned development/test profiles;
- direct dependency requirements are declared but their selected lower bounds are not independently exercised;
- no deterministic base/head receipt explains manifest plus `Cargo.lock` graph movement for dependency review;
- Clippy enforcement is repeated in command lines rather than inherited from one workspace lint authority;
- feature-gated public surfaces exist, but no one matrix states which configurations are actually supported and at what proof depth;
- dependency-feature expansion is not checked against product-scoped selected policy;
- package docs metadata uses a generic workspace URL rather than package-specific docs.rs identity;
- API support tiers do not yet govern compatibility reports;
- untrusted parser coverage remains hand-fixture-led rather than fuzz-backed;
- deterministic Changie/changelog contracts and advisory model review still share one workflow authority;
- static workflow construction/security checking is narrower than the release-action pin inventory;
- the large repository-owned shell/Python control plane has no complete static/runtime contract;
- most non-MSRV CI rows use moving `stable` toolchains and/or `*-latest` runner labels without classifying them as moving observations/canaries;
- coverage uploads exist, but one mixed denominator and provider upload do not define product-scoped coverage evidence or regression policy;
- both macOS rows skip the complete persistent scan-cache test modules because the current path guard rejects the `/var` → `/private/var` platform alias;
- the #2036 incident showed that prose instructing agents to reload current state does not itself prevent stale premise, contradictory prior disposition, irrelevant generated context, or ignored lockfile movement from becoming a merged PR;
- small source and repository hygiene improvements remain unowned or advisory-only.

These are distinct problems. Do not collapse them into one implementation PR.

## Allocated issue train

| Lane | Issue | Primary output |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Current-main issue premise | #3918 | exact issue/source/external-state premise receipt and stale/collision stop route |
| Direct dependency floors | #3903 | selected direct-minimum-version requests and receipts by product/MSRV |
| Dependency graph deltas | #3920 | deterministic manifest/lockfile base-head graph movement and policy adjacency |
| Workspace lint authority | #3904 | inherited Rust/Clippy policy with explicit exc

Write a small self-contained Jac demo program that models the essential scenario from this issue natively: pick the 2-4 core entity types as node archetypes with typed has fields, connect instances with edges (typed edges where the relationship carries meaning), and implement walker(s) that perform the traversal the issue complains about (e.g. recursion over parents/children, cascade delete or invalidation, dependency ordering, cycle detection, reachability). Wire a small demonstration graph in a `with entry` block and spawn the walker(s). No I/O, no external libraries, no pytest blocks.

```jac
"""Modernization gap: declared Rust/dependency/API/workflow contract vs. proven evidence.

The maintainer's complaint is that hand-rolled adjacency (manifest + lockfile +
docs + workflows + CI rows) is walked by ad-hoc scripts, leaving drift between
what the repo *claims* and what it *proves*. Here we model the core entities as
node archetypes, the relationship between a declared contract claim and its
evidence as typed edges, and use a walker to traverse the claim -> evidence
graph to flag claims that lack proof, have stale evidence, or are
policy-adjacent.
"""

# ---- Entity archetypes ----------------------------------------------------

node Claim {
    has title: str = "";
    has lane: str = "";
    has claim_id: str = "";
}

node Evidence {
    has title: str = "";
    has source: str = "";
    has deterministic: bool = False;
}

node Policy {
    has title: str = "";
    has strict: bool = True;
}

node ReviewNote {
    has message: str = "";
}

# ---- Typed edges ----------------------------------------------------------

# A Claim is backed by zero or more Evidence nodes.
edge BackedBy {
    has observed_at: str = "";
    has stale: bool = False;
}

# A Claim is governed by a Policy.
edge GovernedBy { }

# A ReviewNote annotates a Claim with a free-text observation.
edge AnnotatedBy { }

# ---- Walker: traverse claim graph, classify evidence posture --------------

walker ClaimAuditor {
    has proven: list[str] = [];
    has unsupported: list[str] = [];
    has stale_claims: list[str] = [];
    has policy_adjacent: list[str] = [];

    can start with Root entry {
        visit [-->];
    }

    can audit with Claim entry {
        evidence_nodes = [here->:BackedBy:->];
        policy_nodes = [here->:GovernedBy:->];
        note_nodes = [here->:AnnotatedBy:->];

        ev_count = len(evidence_nodes);
        if ev_count == 0 {
            self.unsupported.append(here.title);
            print(f"[UNSUPPORTED] {here.claim_id} {here.title} (lane={here.lane})");
        } else {
            any_deterministic = False;
            any_stale = False;
            for ev in evidence_nodes {
                if ev.deterministic {
                    any_deterministic = True;
                }
            }
            for ev in evidence_nodes {
                if not ev.deterministic {
                    any_stale = True;
                }
            }

            if any_stale {
                self.stale_claims.append(here.title);
                print(f"[STALE] {here.claim_id} {here.title} - evidence exists but marked stale");
            } elif any_deterministic {
                self.proven.append(here.title);
                print(f"[PROVEN] {here.claim_id} {here.title} - deterministic evidence present");
            } else {
                self.unsupported.append(here.title);
                print(f"[UNSUPPORTED] {here.claim_id} {here.title} - non-deterministic evidence");
            }
        }

        if len(policy_nodes) > 0 {
            self.policy_adjacent.append(here.title);
        }
        if len(note_nodes) > 0 {
            for nt in note_nodes {
                print(f"  note: {nt.message}");
            }
        }

        visit [-->];
    }
}

# ---- Demonstration wiring -------------------------------------------------

