A maintainer on the project DarioHuesoGisbert/bonsai (unknown) filed this issue about hand-rolled graph machinery causing pain:

Issue #11: Implement Commit concrete class (history as a Merkle DAG)

## Overview

Implement `Commit`, the third concrete object type in the bonsai Git-like project (namespace `bonsai::objects`). A commit records "this tree is now the state of the repository": it points to a `Tree` by hash, optionally to parent `Commit`(s) by hash, plus author metadata and a message. With it, the object model becomes a real Merkle DAG — a commit's identity chains the identities of its tree and parents, making history tamper-evident and content-addressed, enabling `git log` / `git checkout` (Phases 3–4).

## Use Cases

- Create an initial commit with a tree id, author, message, and no parents.
- Add parents one at a time (oldest first) via `add_parent` during assembly.
- Serialize commits canonically and derive deterministic content-addressed IDs.
- Walk parent chains by hash; changing anything anywhere in history changes the hash of every descendant commit.

## Serialization Format (canonical)

After the `"commit <len>\0"` header (same convention as Blob/Tree; `<len>` is body byte count excluding the header), the body is:

```
tree <hex tree id>\n
[parent <hex parent id>\n]   (repeated once per parent, only if any)
author <name> <email>\n
\n
<message>
```

- Tree line mandatory and first.
- Parent lines only appear if parents exist; initial commit has none.
- No timestamps or timezone (deliberate bonsai simplification for determinism — "same content, same identity"). If timestamps are wanted later, add an explicit `std::string timestamp` field — do **not** invent a default.
- Message separated from header block by `\n\n`; empty message still ends with `author ...\n\n`.
- IDs rendered lowercase hex via `Sha256::hex()`; the commit is hashed over those exact bytes.
- Example with tree `T`, parent `P`, author `A B <a@b>`, message `hi`:
  ```
  tree T
  parent P
  author A B <a@b>

  hi
  ```

## Required Public API

```cpp
namespace bonsai::objects {

struct Author {
    std::string name;
    std::string email;
};

class Commit : public Object {
public:
    Commit(bonsai::hash::Sha256 tree_id, Author author, std::string message);

    // Parents added one at a time, in chronological order (oldest first).
    void add_parent(bonsai::hash::Sha256 parent_id);

    [[nodiscard]] std::string type() const override;                          // "commit"
    [[nodiscard]] std::vector<std::byte> serialize() const override;

    [[nodiscard]] const bonsai::hash::Sha256& tree_id() const noexcept;
    [[nodiscard]] std::span<const bonsai::hash::Sha256> parents() const noexcept;
    [[nodiscard]] const std::string& author_name() const noexcept;
    [[nodiscard]] const std::string& author_email() const noexcept;
    [[nodiscard]] const std::string& message() const noexcept;

private:
    bonsai::hash::Sha256 tree_id_;
    std::vector<bonsai::hash::Sha256> parents_;
    Author author_;
    std::string message_;
};

}  // namespace bonsai::objects
```

Signatures may be refined, but keep: `Commit` is an `Object`; tree line mandatory and first; parents a list; no timestamps; serialization matches the canonical format.

## Acceptance Criteria

- `Commit` inherits from `Object`, overrides `type()` (`"commit"`) and `serialize()`.
- Constructor requires tree id, author, message; parents start empty.
- `add_parent(...)` appends; multiple calls append multiple parents.
- `serialize()` emits canonical `"commit <len>\0<body>"` with exact line structure.
- Initial commit (no parents) has no parent lines; a commit with parents includes one `parent <hex>` line per parent.
- Two commits with same tree/author/parents/message produce equal `id()` (determinism, Template Method via `Object::id()`).
- Changing message, tree id, or a parent produces a different `id()`.
- Chain works: `commit2.parents()[0] == commit1.id()` and `commit2` hashes differently from `commit1`.
- Accessors are `const`, return non-owning views (`span` / `const&`); no mutation after construction except `add_parent` during assembly.
- No raw `new`/`delete`; RAII containers and value types only.
- Clean build with `/W4` (MSVC) and `-Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic -Wconversion` (gcc/clang).
- GoogleTest covers the above cases; CI green on Ubuntu.

